INT. FAIRBANKS FAMILY DINING ROOM - EVENING

A formal dining room in a house that is slightly too large. A chandelier. A mahogany table set for four, with a fifth chair pushed against the wall. A grandfather clock ticks.

LAWRENCE FAIRBANKS-ODUYA (60s, three-piece suit, silver hair brushed back) carves a roast with exacting care. At the other end of the table, MARGARET (60s, pearls, a smile on a timer) arranges napkins. Between them sit their grown children: CLAUDIA (30s, tailored, a wedding ring she keeps turning) and THEODORE "TEDDY" (30s, a rumpled blazer, no tie).

The roast is placed. Lawrence serves Claudia first, then Margaret, then himself. Teddy's plate stays empty.

LAWRENCE
Claudia. Your promotion. Tell us.

CLAUDIA
It's nothing, Father. Just a title.

LAWRENCE
Don't undersell it.

MARGARET
Lawrence, let her eat.

Teddy takes a roll from the basket. He tears it in half and lays both halves on his plate.

LAWRENCE
And you, Theodore?

TEDDY
I'm fine.

LAWRENCE
That wasn't the question.

TEDDY
It was an answer.

Margaret refills her water glass. She does not drink. Claudia glances at her brother; he glances back. The clock ticks.

LAWRENCE
Your mother and I were discussing the lease.

TEDDY
What lease?

LAWRENCE
The office. Downtown. It expires in June.

TEDDY
I know when it expires.

LAWRENCE
Then you know we won't renew.

Teddy sets the roll down. He wipes his fingers on the napkin, folds it into a neat square, and places it on the table.

CLAUDIA
Father, can we not do this tonight?

LAWRENCE
When, then?

MARGARET
(brightly)
More potatoes, anyone?

TEDDY
Thank you, Mother. No.

Lawrence sets down the knife and fork, side by side, at precisely five o'clock. He looks at his son for a long, patient moment.

LAWRENCE
You've had five years.

TEDDY
I've had five years of you watching.

Claudia stands. She begins clearing plates that have barely been touched. Margaret rises, takes the potato dish, and goes to the kitchen without a word. The door swings shut behind her.

Teddy pushes his chair back, takes his blazer from the chair, and stands there with it hanging from one hand.

LAWRENCE
Sit down.

TEDDY
No.

For the first time, Lawrence's hand trembles on the table. He closes it into a fist and holds it there.
